#{[17592186308581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d942a907-7558-5547-92ea-8c390f2de7e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.162.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce506036-afe4-59f4-88ca-ada7f672db57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3+1}={3}^{4}=81.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.162}{81}=0.002.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8aaeaee6-5815-57be-985f-e23b60c35867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186311967 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311967 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311967 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00fa78b3-f990-59e4-9c5d-d0332b6aff1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311980 #edb/ref 1080 "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311980 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311980 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02bc3189-1d45-5e38-b2ab-7948fb5